I'll go with a moderate approach:\\n1. Rebase onto origin/master (fixes staleness).\\n2. Refactor main.go minimally to extract `loadDefaultInventory()` helper that returns (Inventory, error), preserving exact behavior and boundary. Actually, let me keep the rejected diff exactly as it was but add a helper? The rejected diff already changed the else branch to fail loudly. If I now refactor that branch into a helper, the resulting main.go diff relative to master is:\\n\\n```go\\n} else {\\n    loaded, err := loadDefaultInventory(\\\"canopy.json\\\")\\n    if err != nil {\\n        log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)\\n    }\\n    inventory = loaded\\n}\\n```\\n\\nplus helper:\\n```go\\nfunc loadDefaultInventory(path string) (Inventory, error) {\\n    if fi, err := os.Stat(path); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\\n        return LoadInventory(path)\\n    }\\n    return Inventory{}, nil\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWait, but should the helper return an error distinguishing \\\"absent\\\"? Absent is zero-config, not an error. So returning (Inventory{}, nil) for absent is right.\\n\\nThen unit tests in main_test.go (new file):\\n- TestLoadDefaultInventoryAbsentIsZeroConfig: temp dir without canopy.json, call helper with path, expect nil error, zero inventory (Listen empty).\\n- TestLoadDefaultInventoryMalformedFailsLoudly: temp dir with malformed canopy.json, expect non-nil error and contains \\\"decode inventory\\\" or LoadInventory text.\\n- TestLoadDefaultInventoryDirectorySkipped: directory named canopy.json -> zero config (matches existing `!fi.IsDir()`).\\n- TestLoadDefaultInventoryValidLoads: valid file -> inventory loaded.\\n\\nBut testing relative path requires chdir because helper takes path now (I gave it path param) — no chdir needed! Great: helper takes path argument, tests pass absolute paths from t.TempDir(). That's clean and avoids process exec entirely.\\n\\nBut does that test \\\"main default-canopy path\\\"? It tests the helper that main calls. Hmm — is that enough? The verdict said \\\"main default-canopy path has no dedicated test\\\". The actual boundary `log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)` — in the -config path there are presumably no tests either, but the fix path is the default. Testing the helper pins the decision and error propagation. I could also add a test that runs the binary to confirm the observable behavior. Given the fixer directive \\\"add a regression test for an observable defect\\\", I think the helper-level test IS the regression test for the defect: before the fix, errors from LoadInventory were discarded; the test asserts they're surfaced. That's the observable defect at the decision level.\\n\\nHmm, but \\\"observable\\\" implies at the boundary. I think adding the process-level test for the malformed case would be the strongest and also matches Powder's proof. Let me add both? That might be overkill but high-value. Actually, let me not over-engineer. Let me think about the time and risk. The process-level test invokes `go build` within `go test`, which in some CI sandboxes can be slow or problematic, but here it's local and fine.\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider: `go test ./...` already runs the package from the repo root. If I add a process test that runs `go build -o tmp .` inside the test, it will compile the package again. That's fine on this machine.\\n\\nAlternatively, a simpler, fully deterministic approach within `go test` without recompiling: use the extracted helper + unit tests. I'll do that, and also consider whether to run the actual process manually as part of proof (I can do the Powder proof manually via commands, not necessarily as a test).\\n\\nWait — the fixer workflow says: \\\"If a repair check fails, do not commit or publish.\\\" The configured checks are go test and go vet. The regression test should be part of the repo. My helper-level tests are deterministic and robust.\\n\\nDecision:\\n1. Rebase the fix commit onto origin/master.\\n2. Extract the default-inventory load into a helper `loadDefaultInventory(path string)` (name: maybe `loadDefaultInventory`) that keeps behavior identical.\\n3. Add main_test.go with regression tests (absent -> zero config; malformed -> error; directory -> skipped; valid -> loaded).\\n4. Run go test ./... and go vet ./...\\n5. Commit (as the fixer, new commit on top of rebased fix? or amend?).\\n\\nNow about committing: The fixer should \\\"commit the new full SHA\\\". Typically the fixer creates one or more commits on the branch whose tip is the new revision. Since Forest review-request revision must be the full SHA of the branch tip after repair. Options:\\n- Rebase 0b6189b onto origin/master → new commit SHA X (same content as 0b6189b).\\n- Add another commit for the regression test → new tip SHA Y.\\n- Publish revision Y (branch tip). That's fine — the branch tip is the new revision.\\n\\nActually, should the fixer squash everything into one commit? The request/verdict flow uses the branch tip as revision; a fixer typically commits their repairs as new commits on top of the rebased base. I'll create the rebased commit then a test commit, and publish the new tip. The rejection was only staleness + optional test; both are addressed.\\n\\nLet me also double-check the README precedence statement mentioned in the Powder spec: \\\"Align the README precedence statement in `canopy-docs-discovery-alignment` with the corrected behavior before that documentation is completed.\\\" That's a dependency on another branch (canopy-docs-discovery-alignment/document-discovery-precedence) to be done before that documentation is completed — not required for this revision (it says \\\"before that documentation is completed\\\"). The verdict doesn't mention it as blocking. I'll note it but it's not part of this repair contract per the Verdict. The Verdict reasons: stale/diverged (blocking). Optional: no dedicated test. So I need to fix staleness and add the test.\\n\\nLet me check the current README to see if it says anything about default config behavior. If the README already describes the behavior correctly, no change needed. Only the docs branch should align per spec — not in scope for this branch.\\n\\nLet me proceed with the rebase. First, create a branch from the rejected tip? We're on detached HEAD. I can rebase directly. Since we need to publish via `forest publish review-request fixer \\\"$branch\\\" \\\"$payload_file\\\" --rejected \\\"$rejected_sha\\\"`, the forest CLI will handle branch push. Let me check how `forest publish` works — is there a forest binary available?\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Baseline checks pass on the rejected revision; the blocking issue is staleness. Now I'll rebase the fix onto current `origin/master` and add the regression test the verdict flagged as optional."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_a84b81ca48be4b15b086eae0","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /home/phaedrus/development/misty-step/canopy/.forest/worktrees/1788550545620164410-fixer && git rebase --onto origin/master ca9e237 0b6189b 2>&1; echo \"rebase exit=$?\"; git log --oneline -4 HEAD"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash-0731","usage":{"input":175,"output":3831,"cacheRead":37632,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4014,"totalTokens":41638,"cost":{"input":0.000008750000000000001,"output":0.00038310000000000004,"cacheRead":0.00037632000000000003,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0007681700000000001}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1788550662581,"responseId":"gen-1788550662-cV1ZBIqZQ5l1GacC7yNV","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_a84b81ca48be4b15b086eae0","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rebasing (1/1)\rSuccessfully rebased and updated detached HEAD.\nrebase exit=0\n5ddac4f fix(canopy): fail loudly on malformed default canopy.json\nc203dc9 test(canopy): pin audit divergence stale trust override\nf2c0e7b fix(agents): scope critic and tester sweeps to Canopy\nca9e237 feat(canopy): discover forest.yaml checkouts with factory fallback\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1788550682037},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The rebase was clean. Now the new tip SHA is `5ddac4f`. Now I need to add the regression test for the default-canopy path. The verdict's optional note says \"main default-canopy path has no dedicated test.\" The fixer contract says \"add a regression test for an observable defect.\"\n\nThe observable defect: when `-config` is absent and a regular `canopy.json` exists but is malformed, startup should fail loudly. Currently the logic is embedded in `main()`. To write a focused regression test, I could:\n\n1. Test the actual process via os/exec from a temp dir (like the Powder proof).\n2. Refactor the loading into a testable function and test it.\n\nThe simplest regression test that's still meaningful: run the built binary from a temp directory containing a malformed canopy.json, without -config, and assert it exits nonzero with \"load inventory:\" on stderr. That directly tests the observable defect described in the verdict/spec.\n\nBut running `go build`/`go run` in a test is a bit heavy. Let me look at how tests are structured. There isn't a main_test.go yet. Options:\n\nOption A: Add a test that builds the binary once (via TestMain) and runs it in temp dirs. This is a real end-to-end regression test, matching the Powder proof. It's deterministic and test-only.\n\nOption B: Refactor main.go slightly to extract `loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error)` and test that function directly. This is lighter and more unit-testable. The refusal: `log.Fatalf(\"load inventory: %v\", err)` stays in main. But testing main's exact exit behavior isn't captured. However, testing the extracted logic does capture the defect: errors from LoadInventory on default canopy.json propagate rather than being silently discarded.\n\nLet me consider what the verifier would expect. The verdict says \"Optional: main default-canopy path has no dedicated test.\" The request spec proof requires running the actual process. A dedicated test of the default-canopy path would be ideal.\n\nI think the cleanest approach that adds real regression value without over-engineering is to extract the inventory-loading decision into a function and test it, plus add a process-level test using `os/exec` that matches the spec proof (malformed canopy.json -> nonzero exit, stderr contains \"load inventory:\") and the companion case (no file -> starts and /healthz returns 200) — but starting a server in a test requires management. Let me think about the trade-offs.\n\nActually, an `os/exec`-based test is the most faithful \"regression test for an observable defect\" and directly tests main's behavior. Let me write it carefully:\n\n```go\npackage main\n\nimport (\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"path/filepath\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\t\"testing\"\n\t\"time\"\n\t\"net/http\"\n)\n\nfunc buildCanopy(t *testing.T) string {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tbin := filepath.Join(t.TempDir(), \"canopy\")\n\tcmd := exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", \"-o\", bin, \".\")\n\tif out, err := cmd.CombinedOutput(); err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"go build: %v\\n%s\", err, out)\n\t}\n\treturn bin\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but `go test ./...` runs from the package directory (canopy root), so `go build -o` works. In tests, the working dir is the package dir. Good.\n\nTest 1: malformed canopy.json in temp dir; run bin with `-listen` pointing at an ephemeral port (to ensure no server starts); expect nonzero exit and stderr contains \"load inventory:\". Actually the binary calls `log.Fatalf` before starting the server if the file is malformed, so it should exit nonzero quickly. But to prove \"no server starts\", we could use a fixed port and after exit check nothing is listening... simpler: nonzero exit is sufficient evidence plus \"load inventory:\" in stderr.\n\nTest 2: companion run with no file -> starts zero-config and returns 200 from /healthz. This requires starting the process, waiting for health, then killing it. This is more involved. I could use `-listen 127.0.0.1:0`? But then we don't know the port... The server binds to inventory.Listen from CLI override; with :0, the OS picks a port but the app doesn't print it. So we'd need to pick a free port ourselves in the test (e.g., reserve via net.Listen then close) and pass `-listen 127.0.0.1:PORT`. There's a small race but acceptable for a test. Then poll /healthz until 200. Then terminate the process (cmd.Process.Kill / signal).\n\nAlternatively, keep the test lighter: only test the malformed case (the actual defect) with os/exec and add a unit test for the helper if I extract it. But the powder spec Proof wants both runs; not necessarily both need to be in the regression test.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: The fixer contract says \"add a regression test for an observable defect.\" The observable defect is \"malformed default canopy.json silently ignored at startup.\" So the essential regression is: with a malformed default file and no -config, the process exits nonzero with the load inventory: boundary error. A process-level test is exactly right and deterministic (exit status + stderr assertion).\n\nFor the companion \"no file starts zero-config\" — that's about not breaking the absent-file path. A unit test of the extracted helper would cover that. Let me decide on the approach.\n\nApproach: \n1. Keep main.go mostly as-is but extract the default-config decision into a testable helper, OR keep main.go as-is and write a process-level test only.\n\nHmm. The rebased commit already contains the fix diff. If I refactor main.go now, that's an additional change to the same file. The verifier already reviewed the diff and found no blocking correctness defect; the only blocking cause was staleness. The optional note is that the default-canopy path has no dedicated test. So the minimal repair: rebase (done) + add a dedicated test.\n\nI lean toward a process-level test using os/exec for the malformed case + a helper-based unit test for the absent/valid cases. But adding a helper refactor of main.go might be considered \"unrelated behavior\"? No — it's directly related: it makes the default-canopy path testable and is the exact \"dedicated test\" the verdict asked for. However, refactoring main.go to extract a helper can risk changing behavior subtly. Let me look at the current main.go logic:\n\n```go\n} else {\n    if fi, err := os.Stat(\"canopy.json\"); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\n        loaded, err := LoadInventory(\"canopy.json\")\n        if err != nil {\n            log.Fatalf(\"load inventory: %v\", err)\n        }\n        inventory = loaded\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIf canopy.json is absent or is a directory, zero-config. If present regular file, must load or die.\n\nI could extract:\n\n```go\nfunc loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error) {\n    if configPath != \"\" {\n        loaded, err := LoadInventory(configPath)\n        if err != nil {\n            return Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"load inventory: %w\", err)\n        }\n        return loaded, nil\n    }\n    fi, err := os.Stat(\"canopy.json\")\n    if err != nil || fi.IsDir() {\n        return Inventory{}, nil\n    }\n    loaded, err := LoadInventory(\"canopy.json\")\n    if err != nil {\n        return Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"load inventory: %w\", err)\n    }\n    return loaded, nil\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — the error wrapping matters. Currently main does `log.Fatalf(\"load inventory: %v\", err)` where err is the raw LoadInventory error. If helper wraps with \"load inventory: %w\", then main would do `log.Fatalf(\"%v\", err)` — same output. But this changes the code flow somewhat. It's fine.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: if `os.Stat(\"canopy.json\")` returns an error other than not-exist (e.g., permission), the old code silently continues with zero-config. My helper preserves that (err != nil => zero config). Actually old code: if stat err != nil (any), it doesn't enter the if; then continues zero-config. Yes preserved.\n\nGiven the spec proof and the verdict, I think a process-level test is the strongest regression and directly observable. Let me write both:\n\n- `TestMainMalformedDefaultCanopyFailsLoudly` (process-level): build binary, temp dir with malformed canopy.json, run with `-listen 127.0.0.1:0`? Wait: if we pass -listen with a value like 127.0.0.1:0, the code sets inventory.Listen before load? Let me check ordering: in main.go, the load happens BEFORE the listen override is applied. So passing -listen doesn't matter for the malformed case (it fails during load). Actually order: parse flags; load inventory (may fatal); then listenOverride applied. So for malformed file, it fails before listen. So `-listen` any value is fine; we could omit it. But to prove \"no server starts\", the exit code alone suffices (if it fails to load, it never calls ListenAndServe). Then assert nonzero exit code and stderr contains \"load inventory:\".\n\n- `TestMainAbsentDefaultCanopyStartsZeroConfig` : temp dir with no canopy.json, run binary, poll /healthz until 200, then kill. This is the companion run. This one needs a free port. I'll grab a free port by binding :0, closing, then using it. Standard pattern.\n\nActually, wait. Is `os/exec` reliably available? Yes. But `go build` inside the test adds build time (~2-5s). Also, when running `go test ./...` inside the repository, the test binary is in the canopy package dir, and `go build` there will produce the binary. Fine.\n\nBut one concern: `go test ./...` with `-count=1` etc. Also `go vet ./...` — vet doesn't run tests. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: tests run in parallel? These tests aren't marked Parallel, so they run sequentially. `go build` inside the test writes a binary to t.TempDir — unique dirs. Good.\n\nAnother concern: The healthz check. Let me look at server endpoints to see /healthz path. From server_test.go there was TestHealthIsProcessLivenessOnly. Let me check