x(canopy): fail loudly on malformed default canopy.json\" including the helper + tests. But the author would become the fixer? Actually amend keeps the author of the original commit (builder) unless --reset-author; committer would be fixer. That could look odd. \n\nI think the cleanest and most honest approach: a new commit by Iron Forest Fixer on top of the rebased builder fix, titled something like \"test(canopy): cover default canopy.json startup boundary\". This directly addresses the optional note \"main default-canopy path has no dedicated test.\"\n\nBut the refactor of main.go would also be in that commit (extracting loadInventory to make it testable). A commit titled \"test(...)...\" that also refactors main.go is a bit mixed but acceptable — it's test-enabling refactor + tests. Alternatively, keep main.go as-is (no refactor) and write a process-level test using os/exec, which would not require modifying main.go. That is more \"test-only\" but heavier and potentially fragile in the test environment (building a binary inside a test).\n\nHmm. Let me weigh:\n\nProcess-level test pros:\n- No production code changes; purely additive main_test.go.\n- Directly tests the observable boundary (exit code + stderr).\n- Matches the Powder spec proof.\n\nCons:\n- Requires building/running the binary via `go run` or `go build` inside the test; slower; environment-sensitive (needs go toolchain, network? no). `exec.Command(\"go\", \"build\", ...)` in a test can be slow but works. Some CI setups disable it. This worktree has Go installed, so it would pass here.\n- Potential for flakiness: starting server and polling /healthz needs timing.\n\nUnit-level helper refactor pros:\n- Fast, deterministic.\n- Tests error boundary message directly.\n- Small, clean.\n\nCons:\n- Modifies main.go (production) more than strictly necessary.\n- Does not literally verify process exit.\n\nGiven the verdict marks it optional and the Fixer contract says \"add a regression test for an observable defect\", either is acceptable. I'll go with the unit-level refactor + tests because it's robust and directly pins the boundary message, plus covers the zero-config and directory cases. This is the kind of regression test that belongs in this codebase (their tests are unit tests).\n\nWait, actually let me reconsider whether refactoring main.go might be \"unrelated behavior\". It's not: the entire point is to make the default-canopy path testable, which the verdict explicitly requested (\"main default-canopy path has no dedicated test\"). The refactor preserves observable behavior exactly. I'll do a careful extraction.\n\nLet me now write the refactor. Current main.go relevant part:\n\n```go\n\tvar inventory Inventory\n\tif *configPath != \"\" {\n\t\tloaded, err := LoadInventory(*configPath)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tlog.Fatalf(\"load inventory: %v\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tinventory = loaded\n\t} else {\n\t\t// A default canopy.json that exists as a file is explicit operator\n\t\t// configuration and must fail loudly like -config; only an absent\n\t\t// default preserves zero-config discovery.\n\t\tif fi, err := os.Stat(\"canopy.json\"); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\n\t\t\tloaded, err := LoadInventory(\"canopy.json\")\n\t\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\t\tlog.Fatalf(\"load inventory: %v\", err)\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t\tinventory = loaded\n\t\t}\n\t}\n```\n\nNew:\n\n```go\n\tinventory, err := loadInventory(*configPath)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tlog.Fatalf(\"%v\", err)\n\t}\n```\n\nAnd helper:\n\n```go\n// loadInventory resolves the explicit -config inventory, or a default\n// canopy.json when it exists as a regular file. An absent default preserves\n// zero-config discovery. Load failures are wrapped with the `load inventory:`\n// startup boundary shared by the explicit and default paths.\nfunc loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error) {\n\tif configPath != \"\" {\n\t\tloaded, err := LoadInventory(configPath)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"load inventory: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn loaded, nil\n\t}\n\t// A default canopy.json that exists as a file is explicit operator\n\t// configuration and must fail loudly like -config; only an absent default\n\t// preserves zero-config discovery.\n\tif fi, err := os.Stat(\"canopy.json\"); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\n\t\tloaded, err := LoadInventory(\"canopy.json\")\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\"load inventory: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\treturn loaded, nil\n\t}\n\treturn Inventory{}, nil\n}\n```\n\nWait: previously, the error boundary is `load inventory: %v` where err = LoadInventory error which already has context like `read inventory \"canopy.json\": ...`. After wrap, log.Fatalf(\"%v\", err) prints `load inventory: read inventory \"canopy.json\": ...`. Same. \n\nHowever: notice that original code for the default branch used `log.Fatalf(\"load inventory: %v\", err)` where err is the LoadInventory error directly. Same for explicit. So the boundary is identical.\n\nOne subtle behavior difference: What if `*configPath != \"\"` and LoadInventory succeeds? Previously `inventory = loaded`. Now helper returns loaded. Same.\n\nWhat if default file is a directory? Previously `!fi.IsDir()` so skip -> zero config. New code same. Good.\n\nNow tests. Let me add main_test.go.\n\nAlso double-check `err` variable: after `inventory, err := loadInventory(*configPath)` we use err; the subsequent code uses `templates, err := loadTemplates()` with `:=` — since inventory and err already declared, `templates, err := ...` declares templates and reuses err. That's fine (at least one new variable on LHS). Good.\n\nLet me apply the main.go refactor and add the test.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The fix is in place on the rebased commit. Now I need to add a dedicated regression test for the default-canopy path. The verdict said \\\"Optional: main default-canopy path has no dedicated test.\\\"\\n\\nThe spec also calls for proof: \\\"Run the actual Canopy process from a temporary directory with malformed canopy.json, no -config, and a safe ephemeral listen address. It exits nonzero, stderr contains `load inventory:`, and no server starts. A companion run with no file starts zero-config and returns 200 from `/healthz`.\\\"\\n\\nHow to add a regression test that observes this defect? The main function uses log.Fatalf which calls os.Exit, making it hard to test in-process. Options:\\n\\n1. Extract the loading logic into a testable function `loadInventory(configPath string)` and test it.\\n2. Use `os/exec` to build/run the binary in a temp dir - heavier.\\n\\nThe cleanest, most targeted approach that doesn't change production behavior: refactor minimal logic into a small helper and test it. But careful: Fixer says \\\"Do not add unrelated behavior.\\\" Extracting a helper is low-risk and allows testing.\\n\\nActually, let me think about what regression test is most appropriate and minimal:\\n\\nThe observable defect: with no `-config`, an existing but malformed `canopy.json` silently starts empty. The fix: it must terminate startup through `load inventory:` boundary.\\n\\nA good test could:\\n- Create a temp dir with a malformed canopy.json\\n- Run the binary (built via `go run .` or better a test that calls a helper function) with cwd set to temp dir\\n- Assert nonzero exit, stderr contains \\\"load inventory:\\\", and no server waits.\\n\\nRunning actual binary in a test requires building, which is heavy but doable with `go run` or `go build` inside the test using `os/exec`. That matches the spec proof. However tests in this repo are unit tests; there's no precedent. Let me consider refactoring main.go into a `tryLoadInventory` function that returns error, keeping main behavior identical:\\n\\n```go\\nfunc loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error) {\\n    if configPath != \\\"\\\" {\\n        return LoadInventory(configPath)\\n    }\\n    if fi, err := os.Stat(\\\"canopy.json\\\"); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\\n        return LoadInventory(\\\"canopy.json\\\")\\n    }\\n    return Inventory{}, nil\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen main:\\n\\n```go\\ninventory, err := loadInventory(*configPath)\\nif err != nil {\\n    log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nBut this changes the explicit -config error path: currently `log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)` where err is LoadInventory error. If helper wraps error, main prints \\\"load inventory: %v\\\" with a wrapped error. Simplest: helper returns the raw LoadInventory error and main adds the boundary.\\n\\nWait, but for the default path, the error from LoadInventory(\\\"canopy.json\\\") would be e.g. `decode inventory \\\"canopy.json\\\": ...`. main prints `load inventory: decode inventory \\\"canopy.json\\\": ...` — same as explicit -config path. \\n\\nLet me write:\\n\\n```go\\n// loadInventory resolves the explicit -config inventory, or an existing\\n// default canopy.json, or zero-config discovery when no default file exists.\\nfunc loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error) {\\n\\tif configPath != \\\"\\\" {\\n\\t\\treturn LoadInventory(configPath)\\n\\t}\\n\\tif fi, err := os.Stat(\\\"canopy.json\\\"); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\\n\\t\\treturn LoadInventory(\\\"canopy.json\\\")\\n\\t}\\n\\treturn Inventory{}, nil\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAnd main:\\n\\n```go\\ninventory, err := loadInventory(*configPath)\\nif err != nil {\\n    log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis keeps behavior exactly the same (based on review of current main.go, the -config branch and default branch are identical logic).\\n\\nThen a test file main_test.go:\\n\\n```go\\npackage main\\n\\nimport (\\n\\t\\\"os\\\"\\n\\t\\\"path/filepath\\\"\\n\\t\\\"strings\\\"\\n\\t\\\"testing\\\"\\n)\\n\\nfunc TestLoadInventoryDefaultCanopyFailsLoudly(t *testing.T) {\\n\\t// cwd-dependent because loadInventory reads default canopy.json from cwd\\n\\t...\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSince loadInventory uses `os.Stat(\\\"canopy.json\\\")` relative to CWD, tests need to change cwd. Go 1.24+ has `t.Chdir` which is safe and parallel-unfriendly but sequential tests fine. The go.mod says go 1.26.5, so `t.Chdir` is available. Use `t.Chdir(dir)`.\\n\\nTest cases:\\n1. Malformed default canopy.json -> error non-nil, contains \\\"load inventory\\\" boundary? Actually error from helper is raw; main adds boundary. So the helper test would verify err != nil and error mentions decode. To verify exact boundary, better to test main behavior. But testing main's log.Fatalf is awkward.\\n\\nAlternative: put the boundary inside the helper. E.g. helper returns errors already wrapped. Then main just does `log.Fatal(err)`. But that changes the message construction slightly (still same string).\\n\\nHmm. Let me think about what's the best regression test design that's minimal and precise.\\n\\nApproach A: Refactor helper + unit tests that assert:\\n- no default file: (Inventory{}, nil) — zero config.\\n- malformed default: err != nil and contains \\\"load inventory:\\\"? If helper returns raw LoadInventory error, no boundary. But the *observable* defect contract is the process stderr contains \\\"load inventory:\\\".\\n\\nApproach B: Process-level test. Use `go run .`? That compiles each time (slow) but is the true integration test. Actually building once per test run is okay but tests would be slower and could be flaky in restricted environments.\\n\\nGiven \\\"Do not add unrelated behavior\\\" and the optional note \\\"main default-canopy path has no dedicated test\\\", I think a unit-level test of an extracted helper plus asserting the boundary message via a small wrapper is good. But to truly pin the observable contract, maybe the helper should produce the full error message including boundary? That conflates concerns.\\n\\nLet me look at how the verdict phrased it: \\\"Optional: main default-canopy path has no dedicated test.\\\" They'd likely accept any dedicated test of the default-path logic.\\n\\nSimplest robust design that directly tests main's decision boundary without process exec:\\n\\nExtract `loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error)` where errors from LoadInventory are returned raw, and add a function `loadOrDie`? No.\\n\\nActually, we can test main's exit behavior by running the test binary with `-test.run=...`? That's complicated.\\n\\nAlternative: write the test to call the helper and assert:\\n- err == nil and zero inventory for absent file (temp dir with no canopy.json; chdir there)\\n- err != nil for malformed file; error text mentions decode\\n- err != nil for valid-but-invalid inventory (validation error) — e.g., duplicate instances\\n- dir named canopy.json treated as absent (zero config)\\n\\nAnd to test the `load inventory:` boundary, I can extract a wrapper:\\n\\n```go\\nfunc failLoadInventory(err error) {\\n    log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)\\n}\\n```\\nNot worth it.\\n\\nActually a cleaner design: have loadInventory return the already-boundaried error message by wrapping:\\n\\n```go\\nfunc loadInventory(configPath string) (Inventory, error) {\\n\\tif configPath != \\\"\\\" {\\n\\t\\tloaded, err := LoadInventory(configPath)\\n\\t\\tif err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\\\"load inventory: %w\\\", err)\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\treturn loaded, nil\\n\\t}\\n\\tif fi, err := os.Stat(\\\"canopy.json\\\"); err == nil && !fi.IsDir() {\\n\\t\\tloaded, err := LoadInventory(\\\"canopy.json\\\")\\n\\t\\tif err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\treturn Inventory{}, fmt.Errorf(\\\"load inventory: %w\\\", err)\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t\\treturn loaded, nil\\n\\t}\\n\\treturn Inventory{}, nil\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen main:\\n\\n```go\\ninventory, err := loadInventory(*configPath)\\nif err != nil {\\n    log.Fatalf(\\\"%v\\\", err)\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThe printed message is `load inventory: decode inventory ...` for both paths — identical to original output? Original: `log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)` where err = `read inventory \\\"canopy.json\\\": ...`. With the helper wrapping: `fmt.Errorf(\\\"load inventory: %w\\\", err)` then `log.Fatalf(\\\"%v\\\", err)` prints `load inventory: read inventory ...`. Yes, same output. \\n\\nBut careful: `log.Fatalf` takes a format string; `log.Fatalf(\\\"%v\\\", err)` with err wrapping gives `load inventory: <err>`. Good, and the prefix is exactly `load inventory:`.\\n\\nHmm, but there's one nuance: the original explicit -config path used `log.Fatalf(\\\"load inventory: %v\\\", err)`. If the error itself already contains \\\"load inventory:\\\" somewhere (it doesn't), no issue.\\n\\nThis refactor:\\n- Preserves behavior exactly (error messages identical).\\n- Makes the default-path decision testable with error including the boundary `load inventory:`.\\n- Minimal diff.\\n\\nTests (main_test.go):\\n\\n```go\\npackage main\\n\\nimport (\\n\\t\\\"os\\\"\\n\\t\\\"path/filepath\\\"\\n\\t\\\"strings\\\"\\n\\t\\\"testing\\\"\\n)\\n\\nfunc writeDefaultCanopy(t *testing.T, contents string) string {\\n\\tt.Helper()\\n\\tdir := t.TempDir()\\n\\tif contents != \\\"\\\" {\\n\\t\\tif err := os.WriteFile(filepath.Join(dir, \\\"canopy.json\\\"), []byte(contents), 0o600); err != nil {\\n\\t\\t\\tt.Fatal(err)\\n\\t\\t}\\n\\t}\\n\\treturn dir\\n}\\n\\nfunc TestLoadInventoryAbsentDefaultPreservesZeroConfig(t *testing.T) {\\n\\tdir := writeDefaultCanopy(t, \\\"\\\")\\n\\tt.Chdir(dir)\\n\\tinventory, err := loadInventory(\\\"\\\")\\n\\tif err != nil {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"loadInventory(zero config): %v\\\", err)\\n\\t}\\n\\tif inventory.Listen != \\\"\\\" || len(inventory.Instances) != 0 {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"inventory=%+v, want zero-config\\\", inventory)\\n\\t}\\n}\\n\\nfunc TestLoadInventoryDirectoryNamedCanopyIsZeroConfig(t *testing.T) {\\n\\tdir := t.TempDir()\\n\\tif err := os.Mkdir(filepath.Join(dir, \\\"canopy.json\\\"), 0o755); err != nil {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatal(err)\\n\\t}\\n\\tt.Chdir(dir)\\n\\tinventory, err := loadInventory(\\\"\\\")\\n\\tif err != nil {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"loadInventory(dir): %v\\\", err)\\n\\t}\\n\\tif len(inventory.Instances) != 0 {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"inventory=%+v, want zero-config for directory\\\", inventory)\\n\\t}\\n}\\n\\nfunc TestLoadInventoryMalformedDefaultFailsLoudly(t *testing.T) {\\n\\tdir := writeDefaultCanopy(t, `{\\\"instances\\\": [}`)\\n\\tt.Chdir(dir)\\n\\tif _, err := loadInventory(\\\"\\\"); err == nil || !strings.Contains(err.Error(), \\\"load inventory:\\\") {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"loadInventory(malformed default) error=%v, want `load inventory:` boundary\\\", err)\\n\\t}\\n}\\n\\nfunc TestLoadInventoryValidDefaultLoads(t *testing.T) {\\n\\tdir := writeDefaultCanopy(t, `{\\n\\t\\t\\\"instances\\\": [{\\\"id\\\": \\\"local\\\", \\\"label\\\": \\\"Local\\\", \\\"root\\\": \\\"/tmp/forest\\\", \\\"forest\\\": \\\"/usr/bin/forest\\\"}]\\n\\t}`)\\n\\tt.Chdir(dir)\\n\\tinventory, err := loadInventory(\\\"\\\")\\n\\tif err != nil {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"loadInventory(valid default): %v\\\", err)\\n\\t}\\n\\tif len(inventory.Instances) != 1 {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"instances=%d, want 1\\\", len(inventory.Instances))\\n\\t}\\n}\\n\\nfunc TestLoadInventoryExplicitConfigUnchanged(t *testing.T) {\\n\\tpath := writeInventoryFixture(t, `{\\\"instances\\\": [}`)\\n\\tif _, err := loadInventory(path); err == nil || !strings.Contains(err.Error(), \\\"load inventory:\\\") {\\n\\t\\tt.Fatalf(\\\"loadInventory(explicit malformed) error=%v, want `load inventory:` boundary\\\", err)\\n\\t}\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWait: t.Chdir was added in Go 1.24. go.mod says go 1.26.5, so fine.\\n\\nOne concern: `t.Chdir` in tests that might run in parallel — these tests don't call t.Parallel, and other tests might run in parallel? None in this repo call t.Parallel (based on the tests I saw). Even so, t.Chdir only affects the current test's goroutine? Actually t.Chdir changes the working directory for the entire process, but the testing package serializes tests that aren't parallel (it runs them sequentially in one goroutine), and it restores after